    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A professionally poured concrete patio typically costs roughly $6 to $12 per square foot for a plain broom finish and roughly $12 to $25 per square foot for stamped concrete, so a common 16x20 foot patio runs about $1,900 to $3,800 plain or $3,800 to $8,000 stamped. Stained and exposed aggregate finishes usually land in between. Those are typical US installed ranges that vary by region and change with concrete and labor prices; small patios often cost more per square foot because contractors have minimums. Below is a table by size, what drives the price, the hidden costs such as removing an old patio, and when doing it yourself actually saves money.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Much Does a Concrete Patio Cost?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most homeowners pay somewhere between about $2,000 and $6,000 for a new concrete patio, with a typical plain slab in the 200 to 400 square foot range landing near the lower half and a stamped or colored patio of the same size landing toward the top or beyond. Per square foot, the usual installed range is roughly $6 to $12 for broom-finished gray concrete and roughly $12 to $25 for stamped concrete. Those figures include site prep, a gravel base, forms, concrete, finishing and basic cleanup.
          </p>

          <p>
            The cost is driven by four things in roughly this order: the size of the slab, the finish, the site conditions and your local labor market. A flat, easy-to-reach backyard with good soil and a plain finish is the cheapest scenario. A sloped site that needs excavation, a patio behind the house with no truck access, an old slab that has to be broken up and hauled away, and a decorative finish each add to the bill.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Installed Prices</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$6-12</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Plain broom finish, per sq ft</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$12-25</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Stamped, per sq ft</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$2-6</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Old patio removal, per sq ft</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$1-3</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Pro sealing, per sq ft</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Concrete is usually cheaper than pavers or natural stone for the same area and cheaper than most composite decking. It is not the cheapest patio surface overall, since gravel and some DIY options cost less, but it is one of the cheapest permanent ones. If you are weighing it against pavers, our <a href="/pavers-vs-concrete/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">pavers vs concrete</a> comparison covers cost, cracking, repairs and lifespan side by side.
          </p>

          <p>
            One budget note before the details: concrete prices have climbed in recent years, and they vary considerably by region. Use the ranges in this guide as a sanity check on quotes, not as a price to hold a contractor to. The most reliable number is a written quote from a local contractor who has seen your yard.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="by-size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Does a 10x10, 12x12, 16x20 or 20x20 Patio Cost?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The table below applies the typical per-square-foot ranges to common patio sizes, assuming a standard 4-inch slab on a prepared gravel base. The concrete column shows the approximate volume needed, which is useful when you check a quote or plan a DIY pour. A 4-inch slab uses about one cubic yard of concrete for every 81 square feet, before allowing a little extra for waste and uneven ground.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Patio Size</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Area</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Concrete (4 in. slab)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Plain Broom (typical)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Stamped (typical)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">10 x 10 ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">100 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 1.25 cu yd</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$600-1,200+</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$1,200-2,500</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">12 x 12 ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">144 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 1.8 cu yd</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$860-1,730</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$1,730-3,600</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">12 x 16 ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">192 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 2.4 cu yd</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$1,150-2,300</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$2,300-4,800</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">16 x 20 ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">320 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 4 cu yd</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$1,900-3,840</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$3,840-8,000</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">20 x 20 ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">400 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 5 cu yd</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$2,400-4,800</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$4,800-10,000</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Small patios rarely hit the bottom of their range. A 10x10 slab involves almost as much travel, setup, forming and finishing labor as a patio twice its size, and many contractors set a minimum job price. Ready-mix suppliers also often add a short-load fee when you order less than a few cubic yards. It is common for a very small patio to cost noticeably more per square foot than the table suggests.
          </p>

          <p>
            Large patios, on the other hand, often come in at or below the middle of the range, because the fixed costs are spread over more area. If you are deciding between a 12x12 and a 12x16, the extra 48 square feet may cost less than you expect. Run the numbers on the size you actually want before you shrink the design to save money.
          </p>

          <p>
            Thickness matters too. Four inches is standard for a patio that carries people and furniture. If the slab will carry a hot tub, an outdoor kitchen or occasional vehicle traffic, a contractor may recommend a thicker slab or a thickened section, more reinforcement and a better base. That adds concrete and labor, so make sure every quote is for the same thickness.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="finishes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Much More Do Stamped, Stained or Exposed Aggregate Finishes Cost?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A broom finish is the baseline. After the concrete is floated and troweled, the finisher drags a broom across the surface to give it texture and slip resistance. It is quick, durable and the least expensive finish. A plain gray broom-finished patio is not glamorous, but it is easy to repair, simple to seal and the best value per square foot.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Finish</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Installed Cost per Sq Ft</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Notes</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Plain broom</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$6-12</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cheapest, simplest to repair and seal</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Integral color</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Adds roughly $1-3</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Color mixed through the concrete</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Acid or water-based stain</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Adds roughly $2-5</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Applied after the slab cures</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Exposed aggregate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$10-20</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stone exposed at surface; good traction</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Stamped</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$12-25</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Patterned to look like stone, brick or slate</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Stamped concrete costs more because it is labor intensive and time sensitive. The crew adds color, then presses large textured mats into the surface while the concrete is still workable, often with a release agent that adds a second color. A large stamped patio may need extra finishers to keep ahead of the setting concrete. More complex patterns, multiple colors and borders all push the price toward the top of the range.
          </p>

          <p>
            Staining is cheaper than stamping because it is applied to the finished slab and does not require extra crew at the pour. It can transform a plain gray slab, although results vary with the concrete and the stain. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-stain-concrete-patio-to-look-like-stone/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">staining a concrete patio to look like stone</a> covers the DIY version. Exposed aggregate, where the top layer of cement paste is washed off to reveal the stones, sits in the middle and offers good traction around pools.
          </p>

          <p>
            Decorative finishes carry ongoing costs too. Stamped and stained concrete usually need resealing every few years to keep the color and protect the surface, while plain broom-finished concrete can go longer between sealings or be sealed with a long-lasting penetrating product. Factor that into the real cost of the finish, not just the price on pour day.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="drivers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Pushes the Price of a Patio Up?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Site preparation is the biggest variable behind the headline price. A good patio sits on compacted native soil topped with a layer of compacted gravel, commonly around 4 inches, though contractors adjust for local soil and climate. If the site needs grading, the topsoil and roots have to come out, or the ground slopes and needs to be cut or built up, the excavation and base work can add significantly to the bill.
          </p>

          <p>
            Access is next. If the ready-mix truck can back up to the forms, the pour is fast. If the patio sits behind the house with no truck access, the crew has to move the concrete by wheelbarrow, conveyor or a concrete pump. Pump rentals can add several hundred dollars or more to a job, and wheelbarrowing several yards of concrete takes more labor. Ask contractors how they plan to get the concrete to the forms.
          </p>

          <p>
            Reinforcement and joints matter for durability and add modest cost. Many contractors use wire mesh, rebar or fiber-reinforced concrete in patios, and all patios need control joints cut or tooled at regular spacing so cracks form in the joints rather than across the slab. A quote without control joints is a red flag, not a bargain.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Shape, steps and extras</h3>

          <p>
            Curves, multiple levels, built-in steps, seat walls, fire pit pads and footings for a pergola all add forming and finishing time. A simple rectangle is the cheapest shape to pour. If you want a curved edge, expect the price per square foot to rise, and if you want a pergola or roof later, ask now about footings, which are much easier to place before the slab is poured.
          </p>

          <p>
            Region and season round out the list. Labor and concrete prices vary widely between markets, and contractors in cold climates are busiest in late spring and summer. Some jurisdictions also require permits for patios, especially those attached to the house or adding significant impervious area. Check with your building department, because the permit fee and any inspection requirements belong in your budget.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hidden" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Hidden Costs Should You Budget For?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Removing an old patio is the most common surprise. Breaking up and hauling away an existing concrete slab typically adds roughly $2 to $6 per square foot, depending on thickness, reinforcement, access and local disposal fees. A 300 square foot slab can easily add $600 to $1,800 before the new patio starts. Some contractors include removal in their quote and some do not, so ask.
          </p>

          <p>
            Drainage is another. A patio should slope slightly away from the house so water does not pool against the foundation, commonly around 1/8 to 1/4 inch per foot. If the yard naturally drains toward the house, the contractor may need to regrade or add a drain. Fixing that now costs far less than dealing with a wet basement later.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sealing is a smaller line item but worth budgeting for. A professional sealing typically costs around $1 to $3 per square foot, and DIY sealer often costs well under a dollar per square foot in materials. New concrete generally needs to cure before it is sealed, with many sealer makers calling for about 28 days, so check the product label. Our picks for the <a href="/best-concrete-sealer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best concrete sealer</a> cover penetrating sealers for plain patios and film-forming sealers for decorative ones, and our <a href="/concrete-sealer-cost/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete sealer cost</a> guide covers the math.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, budget for the yard. Concrete trucks and wheelbarrows damage lawns, irrigation lines and landscaping on the way to the patio. Restoring sod, moving sprinkler heads and replanting beds are all real costs. So is furniture, lighting and shade, which often cost as much as the patio itself if you buy them new.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Price the patio you will finish, not just the slab</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Removal, permits, drainage, sealing and lawn repair can add 20 percent or more to the slab quote. Put them in your budget on day one so you are not surprised at the end.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="diy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Can You Save Money Pouring a Patio Yourself?
          </h2>

          <p>
            You can, but less than you might think, and the risks are real. Materials for a plain 4-inch patio, including gravel, forms, reinforcement and concrete, often come to roughly a third to half of a professional quote. The savings come from labor, and pouring and finishing concrete is the part of the job that is hardest to learn on your own slab.
          </p>

          <p>
            Concrete waits for no one. Once the truck starts pouring, you have a limited window, often an hour or two depending on temperature, to place, screed, float, edge, joint and broom the surface. On a hot day, it can be less. A 16x20 patio is about four cubic yards, which is a lot to finish for a first-timer, and mistakes in a slab are permanent. Many DIYers do the prep themselves and hire a finisher for the pour.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bagged concrete is realistic only for small slabs. An 80-pound bag yields about 0.6 cubic feet, so a cubic yard takes roughly 45 bags. A 10x10 patio needs more than 50 bags, all mixed and placed quickly enough to finish as one slab. For anything bigger, ready-mix delivered by truck is the practical choice. Our <a href="/best-concrete-mix/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best concrete mix</a> guide covers bagged options for small pours.
          </p>

          <p>
            The DIY sweet spot is a small, simple, rectangular slab with good access, on a cool overcast day, with at least two or three helpers. The pro sweet spot is anything larger, anything decorative, anything on a slope and anything near the house where drainage matters. Stamped concrete in particular is not a good DIY project: it needs specialized tools, timing and experience to look right.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever you decide, do not skip curing. New concrete needs moisture for its first days to gain strength, and a slab that dries too quickly can scale and crack. Our guide to <a href="/how-long-does-concrete-take-to-cure/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how long concrete takes to cure</a> covers when you can walk on it, when to put furniture on it and when to seal it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="quotes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How Do You Get a Fair Patio Quote?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Get at least three written quotes, and make each one specify the same things: area and thickness, base material and depth, reinforcement, finish, control joint spacing, removal of any existing slab, drainage and slope, cleanup and whether sealing is included. When two quotes differ by thousands of dollars, the difference is usually in one of those lines, not in the contractor's margin.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ask about the concrete itself. Exterior slabs in freeze-thaw climates should generally use air-entrained concrete, which contains tiny air bubbles that help resist frost damage. Ask what strength mix the contractor uses and whether it is air-entrained if you live where it freezes. A contractor who cannot answer is a contractor to pass on.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ask about curing and warranty. Who is responsible for keeping the slab moist in its first days, and how long should you stay off it? What does the contractor warranty, and for how long? Hairline shrinkage cracks are common in concrete and rarely covered, but major cracks, settling and surface failure in the first year or two often are.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, check references and look at older work. A patio that looks good on pour day can look very different after three winters. Ask to see a job the contractor did a few years ago, check that they are licensed and insured where required, and avoid paying most of the price up front. A reasonable deposit followed by payment on completion is standard.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skimping on the base</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Concrete is only as good as what it sits on. Pouring over soft soil, topsoil or uncompacted fill invites settling and cracking. A layer of compacted gravel, commonly around 4 inches, over compacted subgrade costs relatively little and does more for the patio's lifespan than any finish or sealer you could add later.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting control joints</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Concrete shrinks as it cures and moves with temperature, and it will crack. Control joints, cut or tooled at regular intervals, tell it where to crack. A patio without them cracks randomly across the slab. Make sure every quote includes control joints and the spacing the contractor plans to use.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sloping the patio toward the house</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A patio needs a slight fall away from the foundation, commonly about 1/8 to 1/4 inch per foot. A flat or reversed slope sends rain toward the house and invites basement leaks and ice. Check the planned slope before the forms are set, not after the concrete has hardened.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing new concrete too early</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Fresh concrete still holds a lot of moisture. Sealing it too soon can trap that moisture and cause a white haze or poor bonding with film-forming sealers. Many manufacturers call for about 28 days of curing first, though some products are made for new concrete. Always follow the label.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Underestimating DIY finishing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pouring is the easy part. Screeding, floating, edging, jointing and brooming several yards of setting concrete takes skill and speed, and mistakes are permanent. If you DIY, keep the slab small, pour on a cool day with helpers, or do the prep yourself and hire a finisher for pour day.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a concrete patio cheaper than pavers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually, yes. Plain poured concrete typically costs roughly $6 to $12 per square foot installed, while concrete pavers commonly run higher because each unit is laid by hand on a prepared base. Stamped concrete can approach or overlap paver pricing. Pavers are easier to repair one piece at a time, so the cheaper upfront choice is not always cheaper over decades.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a concrete patio last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A well-built concrete patio commonly lasts 25 to 30 years or more. The biggest factors are base preparation, drainage, control joints, the concrete mix and your climate. Freeze-thaw cycles and deicing salts shorten its life, while periodic sealing and keeping water from pooling on or under the slab help extend it. Decorative finishes may need refreshing sooner than the slab itself.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a permit for a concrete patio?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on where you live. Some jurisdictions require permits for any new patio, others only for patios attached to the house, above a certain size, or that change drainage or impervious surface area. Covered patios and those with footings for a roof almost always need one. Call your local building department before you pour, since fixing an unpermitted slab later can be expensive.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How thick should a concrete patio be?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Four inches is the standard thickness for a patio that carries foot traffic and furniture. Thicker slabs or thickened sections are often recommended for hot tubs, outdoor kitchens, heavy planters or areas that may see vehicles. The base matters too: a compacted gravel layer beneath the slab supports it and helps drainage. Make sure all quotes specify the same thickness and base.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I seal a new concrete patio?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sealing is optional for plain concrete but recommended in freeze-thaw climates and for stamped or stained patios. A sealer reduces water and stain absorption and protects decorative color. Many manufacturers recommend waiting about 28 days after the pour, though some products are formulated for newer concrete. Plan to reseal every few years with film-forming sealers, less often with penetrating ones.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a concrete patio add value to a home?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A patio can make a home more appealing to buyers, especially in regions with long outdoor seasons, but it rarely returns its full cost at sale. The best value comes from a patio that is well built, sized to the yard and in good condition. A cracked, stained or poorly drained patio can count against a home, so maintenance matters as much as the original build.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">When is the best time of year to pour a patio?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Mild weather is best. Many contractors prefer temperatures roughly between 50 and 80 degrees Fahrenheit, which let concrete set at a manageable pace and cure without extra protection. Very hot weather speeds evaporation and shortens finishing time, while cold weather slows curing and risks freezing. In cold climates, late spring and early fall are common choices, with summer pours done early in the morning.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

